Let $O$ be the origin, and let the circle intersect the $y$-axis at points $R$ and $S.$

[asy]
unitsize(3 cm);

pair A, O, R, S, T;

real func (real x) {
  return ((x - 1)*(x - 2));
}

A = (1.5,-0.4);
O = (0,0);
R = intersectionpoint(Circle(A,abs(A - (1,0))),(-0.5,0)--(0,0.5))[0];
S = intersectionpoint(Circle(A,abs(A - (1,0))),(-0.5,0)--(0,0.5))[1];
T = intersectionpoint(Circle(A,abs(A - (1,0))),arc(A/2,abs(A)/2,0,90));

draw(graph(func,0.5,2.5));
draw((-0.5,0)--(2.5,0));
draw((0,-1)--(0,1));
draw(Circle(A,abs(A - (1,0))));
draw(O--T);
draw(R--S);

label("$t$", T/3, N);
label("$O$", O, SW);
label("$p$", (1,0), S);
label("$q$", (2,0), S);
label("$R$", R, W);
label("$S$", S, E);

dot(T);
[/asy]

Then by Power of a Point, $t^2 = OR \cdot OS.$

Since the parabola intersects the $x$-axis at $(p,0)$ and $(q,0),$
\[ax^2 + bx + c = a(x - p)(x - q).\]Setting $x = 0,$ we get $c = apq.$

Also, $R$ and $S$ are the intercepts of the circle with the $y$-axis.  Since the circle passes through $(p,0)$ and $(q,0),$ the center of the circle lies on the line $x = \frac{p + q}{2}.$  Let the center of the circle be $\left( \frac{p + q}{2}, k \right).$  Then the radius of the circle is $\frac{p + q}{2},$ so
\[\left( \frac{p + q}{2} \right)^2 + k^2 = \left( \frac{p + q}{2} \right)^2.\]Hence, $k = 0,$ so the circle passes through the origin.  Then $OR \cdot OS = 0,$ so $t^2 = \boxed{0}.$
